%S A399135 384,1890,1920,3456,21000,21870,24576,25000,46656,376320,429000,
%T A399135 432000,450450,497664,810000,1327104,1344000,1646400,1786050,2621440,
%U A399135 3188646,3456000,3763200,4537890,4920750,7203000,8588580,8605184,8824530,9135126,10536960,13781250,21000000
%N A399135 Numbers for which repeatedly subtracting the greatest prime factor (A006530) to reach 0 takes fewer steps than repeatedly subtracting the squarefree kernel (A007947).
%C A399135 Numbers k such that A309892(k) < A255326(k).
%C A399135 The greatest-prime-factor subtraction process (A309892) and the radical subtraction process (A255326) start from the same n. Since rad(n) >= gpf(n), the radical removes at least as much per step, so heuristically the radical process should never take more steps; this sequence lists the integers where that heuristic fails.
%C A399135 All terms are nonsquarefree, since if n is squarefree then rad(n) = n and the radical process reaches 0 in just a single step.
%H A399135 Michael S. Branicky, <a href="/A399135/b399135.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..38</a>
%e A399135 384 = 2^7 * 3 is a term since the greatest-prime-factor process is 384 -> 381 -> 254 -> 127 -> 0 (4 steps), while the radical process is 384 -> 378 -> 336 -> 294 -> 252 -> 210 -> 0 (6 steps), and 4 < 6.
%t A399135 gpfStep[n_] := n - FactorInteger[n][[-1, 1]];
%t A399135 radStep[n_] := n - Times @@ FactorInteger[n][[All, 1]];
%t A399135 steps[n_, f_] := Length@NestWhileList[f, n, # > 0 &] - 1;
%t A399135 Select[Range[2, 50000], steps[#, gpfStep] < steps[#, radStep] &]
%o A399135 (Python)
%o A399135 from sympy import primefactors
%o A399135 def rad(n):
%o A399135     r = 1
%o A399135     for p in primefactors(n): r *= p
%o A399135     return r
%o A399135 def steps(n, f):
%o A399135     c = 0
%o A399135     while n > 0:
%o A399135         n -= f(n); c += 1
%o A399135     return c
%o A399135 def ok(n):
%o A399135     return steps(n, lambda m: max(primefactors(m))) < steps(n, rad)
%o A399135 print([n for n in range(2, 50001) if ok(n)])
%Y A399135 Cf. A006530, A007947, A255326, A309892, A175126.
%Y A399135 Subsequence of A013929.
